import{_ as a,o as t,c as o,a6 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BUF_B7sW.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"JAX runtime and derivatives","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"jax-runtime.md","filePath":"jax-runtime.md","lastUpdated":null}'),i={name:"jax-runtime.md"};function r(n,e,d,c,l,h){return t(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="JAX-runtime-and-derivatives" tabindex="-1">JAX runtime and derivatives <a class="header-anchor" href="#JAX-runtime-and-derivatives" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;JAX runtime and derivatives {#JAX-runtime-and-derivatives}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This guide continues <a href="./jax">JAX source</a>: runtime checks on accelerators, derivative rules in emitted modules, stable rewrites, and ODE solves.</p><h2 id="Accelerator-runtime-checks" tabindex="-1">Accelerator runtime checks <a class="header-anchor" href="#Accelerator-runtime-checks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Accelerator runtime checks {#Accelerator-runtime-checks}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Data-dependent checks use <code>jax.debug.callback</code>: their predicates are computed on the numerical device and checked on the host. JAX needs a local CPU device for these callbacks, even for valid inputs on CUDA. Start the Python process with <code>JAX_PLATFORMS=cuda,cpu</code>; <code>JAX_PLATFORMS=cuda</code> alone fails when a runtime check executes. CPU-only execution remains supported.</p><p>An explicit platform list makes CUDA initialization mandatory and CUDA the default; it does not silently fall back to CPU if CUDA initialization fails. For a GPU-required consumer, select <code>gpu = jax.devices(&quot;gpu&quot;)[0]</code>, place numerical inputs with <code>jax.device_put(value, gpu)</code>, and verify result and gradient arrays with <code>array.devices() == {gpu}</code>. Evaluate inside <code>with jax.default_device(gpu):</code> so newly created constants use that device too. Numerical function results, including constant terms and numeric leaves of tuple/dict results, are JAX arrays with the emitted dtype. <code>prepare_data</code>, <code>dimension</code>, <code>parameters</code> and module metadata remain host values. The host validates the check predicates; it does not evaluate the numerical kernel. Call <code>jax.block_until_ready(result)</code> and <code>jax.effects_barrier()</code> when checking completion and callback errors. Host callbacks incur transfer and synchronization costs; <code>prepare_data</code> also runs on the host.</p><p>See JAX&#39;s <a href="https://docs.jax.dev/en/latest/external-callbacks.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">callback semantics</a> and <a href="https://docs.jax.dev/en/latest/config_options.html#platforms" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">platform configuration</a>. <code>python benchmark/jax_runtime_checks.py</code> verifies the helper&#39;s values, gradients, device placement, invalid-input rejection and missing-GPU failure with a CUDA-enabled JAX installation.</p><h2 id="Derivative-rules" tabindex="-1">Derivative rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#Derivative-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Derivative rules {#Derivative-rules}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For <code>scalar_derivative_rule</code>, the module generates a <code>jax.custom_jvp</code> adapter from the rule&#39;s mathematical graph. It selects the primal and named partials for the active arguments, then applies the scalar chain rule. An inactive argument&#39;s partial is not contracted with a numeric zero. Calls share their generated helpers, including a joint recipe that serves several activity patterns. Host data preparation uses the primal cut.</p><p>General <code>derivative_rule</code> calls generate adapters from the authored forward branch and activity-selected staged reverse branches. Ordinary <code>jax.grad</code> and <code>jax.value_and_grad</code> compute the joint primal and residuals once, then contract the selected reverse cut. JAX does not differentiate the primal&#39;s convergence loop in place of the supplied derivative. Forward mode uses the authored forward branch; a missing branch or differentiation of a declared data-only input raises. Batching maps whole calls and retains each lane&#39;s lazy branches. These adapters use <code>jax.experimental.hijax.HiPrim</code> and are tested with JAX 0.12.0.1. Only modules containing a general rule import that API.</p><p>Ragged array ports carry their logical lengths through primal, forward and reverse cuts. Lengths have zero tangent; padded cotangents do not contribute to upstream gradients. Runtime checks inside a rule execute only for active calls, including rules reached through a lazy branch or an empty plate. Rules containing array regions retain their source plans as build-time metadata. Native invocation reads only the compiled body and its concrete execution tables.</p><p>The incomplete-beta continued fraction used by distribution tails supplies its value and shape/argument partials through such a shared graph. A first JAX gradient retains those partials rather than a reverse tape of all 300 fraction iterations. The primal and partials use fixed loops, so their higher derivatives remain available through ordinary JAX differentiation.</p><h2 id="Stable-rewrites" tabindex="-1">Stable rewrites <a class="header-anchor" href="#Stable-rewrites" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stable rewrites {#Stable-rewrites}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Unless <code>simplify = false</code>, each emitted recipe computes numerically fragile compositions in the stable form native preparation computes, together with the statements that compute its inputs, also from a lazy arm or a plate&#39;s cell: <code>log(exp(u))</code> is emitted as <code>u</code>, and <code>log1p(-p)</code> of <code>p = logistic(u)</code> reads <code>u</code>, so the value and <code>jax.grad</code> stay finite where <code>exp(u)</code> overflows. A law&#39;s term on a value computed outside the law (<code>Normal(0, s)</code>&#39;s <code>log(s)</code> of <code>s = exp(a)</code>) is still translated as written: the law&#39;s endpoint is translated from its compiled lowered code, which the rewrites do not read yet.</p><h2 id="ODE-solves" tabindex="-1">ODE solves <a class="header-anchor" href="#ODE-solves" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;ODE solves {#ODE-solves}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A call of the native ODE bridges <code>rk_ode_rk45_tol(f, y0, t0, ts, reltol, abstol, maxsteps, args...)</code> or <code>rk_ode_bdf_tol</code> with the same arguments, also inside a plate cell and through a helper such as BayesianRegressionModels&#39; <code>ode_rk45</code>, is one <a href="https://docs.kidger.site/diffrax/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">diffrax</a> solve. A module that solves an ODE imports <code>diffrax</code> (<code>pip install diffrax</code>); other modules do not. Emitting needs none of the Julia solver packages the native bridges load. The right-hand side <code>f(t, y, args...)</code> is translated like any helper into a nested Python function, and the extra arguments that are runtime values reach it through diffrax&#39;s <code>args</code>.</p><ul><li><p><code>rk_ode_rk45_tol</code> is <code>diffrax.Dopri5()</code>, the Dormand–Prince 5(4) method of the native <code>DP5()</code>. <code>rk_ode_bdf_tol</code> is <code>diffrax.Kvaerno5()</code>, diffrax&#39;s stiff (L-stable implicit Runge–Kutta) method: diffrax has no BDF method, so the stiff solve uses a different method than the native <code>FBDF()</code>, with the same tolerances.</p></li><li><p>The caller&#39;s tolerances are passed unchanged and may be traced (data or parameters). The step size follows a PID controller; outputs come from the solver&#39;s dense interpolant, so the native and JAX solves agree to the tolerances, not to the last bit.</p></li><li><p><code>maxsteps</code> limits the accepted steps between consecutive requested outputs, as natively, and may be traced too. diffrax&#39;s own static bound on attempted steps is the largest int32, which costs nothing: a gradient keeps 64 checkpoints whatever the bound.</p></li><li><p><code>jax.grad</code> differentiates the discrete solve (diffrax&#39;s <code>RecursiveCheckpointAdjoint</code>, 64 checkpoints a solve), including with respect to the initial and output times, so a gradient&#39;s accuracy follows the caller&#39;s tolerances, as the native forward sensitivities&#39; does. A backward-solve adjoint such as <code>jax.experimental.ode.odeint</code>&#39;s does not: on a seven-state pharmacokinetic model its gradient was about 35 times less accurate at the same tolerances.</p></li><li><p>Under <code>jax.vmap</code> (a sampler&#39;s chains, or a plate&#39;s cells) one lane&#39;s solve never changes another lane&#39;s value: the batch steps until its slowest lane is done and masks the others&#39; extra steps. A lane&#39;s gradient is independent of the other lanes only up to rounding. diffrax keeps a batch&#39;s backward-pass checkpoints in lockstep, so the lane that needs the most steps decides which steps the backward pass recomputes, and on CUDA XLA&#39;s autotuner can compile those recomputed steps to round differently from the forward ones. A lane&#39;s gradient then changes in its trailing digits when another lane, valid or failed, needs more steps than it does. The autotuner chooses anew in each process, so one program&#39;s gradients also differ between processes, and a point evaluated alone differs from the same point in a batch, by about as much. On a density with a five-state solve in each of 24 plate cells (float64, tolerances <code>1e-8</code>) each of these differences was at most <code>2.1e-4</code> on gradient entries up to <code>2e3</code>, a fifth of the difference between the JAX and native gradients. Compare such gradients with a tolerance, not bitwise. With <code>XLA_FLAGS=--xla_gpu_autotune_level=0</code> that density&#39;s gradients were identical across processes and each lane&#39;s was bitwise independent of its neighbours, as on CPU.</p></li><li><p>A ragged row of output times solves to the row&#39;s last time; its padded rows repeat that time&#39;s state, a column <code>sol[:, j]</code> keeps the padding, and reductions mask it.</p></li><li><p>Invalid inputs (non-finite or non-increasing times, invalid tolerances or <code>maxsteps</code>) raise at run time through the module&#39;s runtime checks, as the native bridge throws. A row without output times raises nothing.</p></li><li><p>An exceeded per-output budget and a failed solve are numerical failures, natively <code>ReactiveKernels.ODESolveFailure</code>. A solve fails where its step size falls below 16 ulps of the latest time, or where it steps from a non-finite state or its error estimate is not finite, as the native solve stops as <code>DtLessThanMin</code> or <code>Unstable</code>; a finite-time blow-up therefore fails at once instead of rejecting steps until diffrax&#39;s step limit. The failure is a <code>jax.experimental.checkify</code> check, which leaves every <code>vmap</code>, <code>scan</code>, <code>cond</code> and loop as a value: the functions <code>jax_source</code>&#39;s <code>rejecting</code> names answer <code>-inf</code> for the whole evaluation, and the module&#39;s other functions raise. The module&#39;s other runtime checks are <code>checkify</code> checks too, so the first failure in program order decides, as the native program stops at its first throw: a check on a failed solve&#39;s state (a solve continuing from it) neither raises nor hides the failure. ReactiveKernelsPPL&#39;s density functions (<code>log_density</code> and its parts) are such functions, as its native sampler targets answer <code>-Inf</code>. Their gradient there is unspecified.</p></li><li><p>A module with an ODE solve therefore needs a JAX whose <code>checkify</code> has <code>checkify.Error.has_error</code> and differentiates a <code>custom_vjp</code> (every diffrax solve) after a check. No JAX release has either yet: with jax 0.12.0.1 such a gradient is wrong or fails (<code>benchmark/repro_jax_checkify_custom_vjp_control_flow.py</code>). Branch <code>jax-v0.12.0.1-checkify</code> of <a href="https://github.com/nsiccha/jax" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">nsiccha/jax</a> carries both, and the module refuses to import without them.</p></li></ul>',16)])])}const m=a(i,[["render",r]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
